  "query_preview": "..FOR EXTREME SOUTHWESTERN CALIFORNIA INCLUDING ORANGE...\nSAN DIEGO...WESTERN RIVERSIDE AND SOUTHWESTERN SAN BERNARDINO\nCOUNTIES...\n\n\n...UPDATED AVIATION DISCUSSION...\n\nSanta Ana winds have been observed this morning. East flow around an \narea of high pressure has resulted in gusts near 30-45 MPH across \nthe San Diego County mts/foothills. Gusty winds and lower relative \nhumidity each day will lead to elevated to near-critical fire \nweather conditions, so make sure to do your part that leads to one \nless spark! East winds have even made it to the coast this morning, \nshooting temperatures up into the 90s along the beaches. Onshore \nwinds will start to take hold this afternoon, knocking temperatures \ndown 5-10 degrees. \n\nHot weather will continue into early next week, as an area of high \npressure anchors over our region. Today and Sunday will likely be \nthe warmest days as weaker offshore flow prevails. The high will \nweaken slightly and move more toward the southwest as we go into the \nearly part of next week. This will allow increased onshore flow \ncloser to the coast, bringing a few degrees of cooling. Nights will \nstill remain warm for many areas in the lower to mid 70s west of the \nmountains. The mountains and deserts will still be warm for this \ntime of year with highs near 5 to 10 degrees above normal, but not \nas intense as places west of the mountains. Mountain areas like \nJulian and Big Bear will be in the 70s and 80s for most of this \nevent. Areas 3000-6000 ft will also see low temperatures well above \nnormal. Please make sure to be in areas of A/C and check on those \nwithout effective cooling. \n\nBy Friday, most models begin to diverge on the high starting to \nweaken as a trough to the north begins to enter the Pacific NW. The \nprogression of this trough is still uncertain, and will help \ndetermine how cool we get by the end of next week. By Friday, \ngreater onshore cooling looks to spread inland helping to lower \ntemperatures mainly into the 80s and 90s west of the mountains. \nFurther cooling may occur if the trough is strong enough to push \nfurther south into California by next weekend.",
